WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.580
- This is the Hill House
in south-west Scotland. 

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.056
It was built in 1902 using design
and construction techniques that were

00:00:06.056 --> 00:00:07.610
years ahead of their time.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.750
It's an important bit of
architectural history,

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.870
but because it was
years ahead of its time,

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.500
it was also experimental.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.650
It turns out that the house
exterior just soaks up all the

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.810
heavy Scottish rains,
and lets the water in.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.670
120 years after it was built,
this whole building

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.520
is slowly cracking and
crumbling and dissolving.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.180
The first step of saving
this house is to dry it out,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.690
slowly and carefully.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:32.200
The problem is, it rains
190 days a year around here.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.250
So the National Trust for Scotland

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.352
had to build a very big drying room.

00:00:37.352 --> 00:00:40.520
- The Hill House is so important.
It is globally known.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.680
We attract visitors
from all over the world

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.610
in a pilgrimage to come
and visit the Hill House.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.283
Charles Rennie Mackintosh is definitely

00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:49.530
one of Scotland's most famous
architects and designers.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.240
It is Mackintosh's domestic masterpiece

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.130
and really is so ahead of its time.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.000
When you walk through the front door,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.040
when you picture what was
essentially a Victorian family

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.240
living in this house, you
realise how forward-thinking

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.393
Mackintosh was in his design.

00:01:02.393 --> 00:01:05.701
He used a material called
Portland cement on the exterior.

00:01:05.701 --> 00:01:08.550
There is no lime at all
used on site as well.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.850
We're located in one of the
wettest parts of Scotland

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:14.300
in Helensburgh so it really
was a recipe for disaster.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.580
The Hill House has been described

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.410
as "dissolving like an
aspirin in a glass of water".

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:22.170
The box basically buys us some
time to dry the house out,

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.530
shelter it from around
86% of the elements.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.000
It acts like a giant umbrella

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.900
and shields the house from the weather.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.840
- The walls in this box
are made of chainmail.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.960
If you go and order a
modern armour costume

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.020
with a chainmail vest, it will
be made of this same stuff.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.770
It's more than 30 million
rings of stainless steel

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.010
linked together, weighing eight tonnes.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.821
The roof catches most of the
rain and the chain mail catches

00:01:44.821 --> 00:01:46.970
what blows in from the sides,

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.480
but it also lets air
through for ventilation.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.530
It lets bees and other insects through
to pollinate the plants in here.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.710
And it's all recyclable
afterwards because it's steel.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.040
You just melt it down into something else.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.090
- The box is made up of 32.4 million
individual chain mail links

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.330
that surround the house,
in the worlds largest sheet of chain mail.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.637
In terms of construction,
it took nine months.

00:02:08.637 --> 00:02:13.247
It really is one of a kind.
It costs around £4,500,000.

00:02:13.247 --> 00:02:14.877
If you were to cover the house completely,

00:02:14.877 --> 00:02:17.347
it would be at risk of suffering
from environmental shock

00:02:17.347 --> 00:02:19.457
and it would cause more damage than good.

00:02:19.457 --> 00:02:23.077
The box style allows the house to breathe,
that is really important.

00:02:23.077 --> 00:02:25.777
It would dry out too quickly
and it would cause damage

00:02:25.777 --> 00:02:29.967
to the exterior and, indeed, to the collection
inside the property as well.

00:02:29.967 --> 00:02:31.334
Before, you were able to visit the house,

00:02:31.334 --> 00:02:33.487
and it was here on its own
and it was amazing,

00:02:33.487 --> 00:02:36.007
but the box adds another level.

00:02:36.007 --> 00:02:37.817
There are walkways all
around the property.

00:02:37.817 --> 00:02:39.507
You can walk around the
perimeter of the house,

00:02:39.507 --> 00:02:41.137
even up and over the top,

00:02:41.137 --> 00:02:43.487
seeing the house from aspects
that Mackintosh himself

00:02:43.487 --> 00:02:44.757
would never even have seen.

00:02:44.757 --> 00:02:47.907
What you're seeing at the Hill House
is conservation in action.

00:02:47.907 --> 00:02:51.050
- They didn't need to add the
staircases and gantries and ramps here.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.957
But if you block this
house from public view

00:02:52.957 --> 00:02:55.907
for the 15 years it'll
take to dry out and repair,

00:02:55.907 --> 00:02:57.927
then there's a chance the public
are going to forget about it.

00:02:57.927 --> 00:03:00.207
It'll look like a
construction site, an eyesore.

00:03:00.207 --> 00:03:01.677
If instead you highlight it,

00:03:01.677 --> 00:03:04.317
you create something
one-of-a-kind like this box

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.777
and you let the public
see the house from angles

00:03:06.777 --> 00:03:09.247
that will likely never be possible again...

00:03:09.247 --> 00:03:12.047
well, then, you're not just
preserving important architecture.

00:03:12.047 --> 00:03:12.880
You're promoting it.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.067
And for me, that's the
cleverest part of all this.

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:17.439
I wouldn't be here,
you wouldn't be watching this,

00:03:17.439 --> 00:03:18.740
if it wasn't for the box.

00:03:32.857 --> 00:03:34.275
It was built in 1902--

00:03:34.275 --> 00:03:35.546
[laughs]

00:03:35.546 --> 00:03:38.260
There's one more step than
I thought there was there!

